Before anonymisation, the dataset contains several fields that could identify someone directly, like their name, email address, and employee ID. 
Salary isn’t an identifier on its own, but it’s still sensitive information, especially in small teams where people might know each other’s pay. 
Because of this, the original dataset carries a moderate level of privacy risk.
After anonymisation, most of that risk is removed. Names are fully redacted, emails are hashed, and employee IDs are replaced with tokens that don’t link back to real people. 
Salary values are encrypted so they’re no longer visible in plain text. Department is left as‑is because it’s low sensitivity and needed for testing.
There is still some residual risk, mainly because anonymised data can sometimes be linked back to individuals if someone has access to external information. 
For example, a very unusual salary or a rare department could still narrow down who someone is in a small organisation.
Hashing also keeps patterns, meaning two identical emails will produce the same hash — useful for testing, but still something to be aware of.
Overall, the anonymisation methods reduce the dataset’s risk from medium to low. 
The output is suitable for testing and development without exposing personal details, as long as it isn’t combined with other identifying information.
  
